+/// The descriptor number the crate's `F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC` duplicates start at.
+///
+/// Every one of these duplicates is handed to the compositor or to the GPU
+/// driver as a number, and both of them read the number out of THIS process's
+/// descriptor table, which is where the vendor EGL driver keeps its own
+/// objects: the measured session holds about a hundred live numbers, `0`
+/// naming `anon_inode:sync_file`, `4` naming `/dev/galcore`, and the driver's
+/// 21 `anon_inode:[eventfd]` entries spread through the low teens. Asking for
+/// the lowest free number (the `, 3` of the compositor handoff and the `, 0`
+/// of the Vulkan imports) put every one of the streamer's exports inside that
+/// band and recycled it 55 times a second, and the release FENCE the driver
+/// had just made for a swapchain image came back to the compositor as one of
+/// them:
+///
+///   zwp_linux_surface_synchronization_v1@30: error 0: invalid fence fd
+///   The Wayland connection experienced a fatal error: Protocol error
+///
+/// which is the interface connection going away 3 min into the first session
+/// long enough for a player to be inside the game, with the two sibling
+/// deaths of the same collision read from the other side
+/// (`buffer_fenced_release: Assertion buffer->release_fence_fd == -1 failed`
+/// and libsystemd's `Assertion close_nointr(fd) != -EBADF failed at
+/// .../fd-util.c:75, function safe_close()`). Nothing in the driver or in
+/// libEGL allocates this high, so the two stop taking each other's number,
+/// and because the previous export was released before the next one is made,
+/// the exports reuse the same handful of numbers instead of walking the table.
+#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
+const EXPORT_FD_BAND: i32 = 4096;
+
 #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
 mod audio;
 #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
